[RUN] The v2 primary top-1 bootstrap interval lies wholly above the unchanged
constant floor. The v2 level interval crosses zero, but the fixed reading needs
only either top-1 or level `R²` to move materially; both point estimates moved,
and top-1 did so decisively.

## Same-sample construction and power

[RUN] The modeled panel is identical to v1: 37 anchors, 36,360 rows, 3,700
`(anchor,user)` groups, and 160,444 non-tied within-group target pairs. It uses
world 1 steps 0–17 and world 2 steps 0–18, setting `a-r0`, carrier
`nearest-eligible`, 100 users, the shipped shortlist support, the same
calibration, and boundary indices `(0,)`. Thus there is no unequal-sample
comparison and no new anchor shortfall. The broader generation cache still has
39 anchors (19 and 20 by world); as in v1, the final two are verification-only
and excluded from modeling.

[RUN] The v2 fitted panel was first made by applying the successor's deterministic
geometry projection to the exact-path v1 parent rows used by the v1 fit. The
parent input digests are `328ca5b2…56aae9` and `d3db1339…f248de`; their exact
base gauge is `0.0`, target recomposition residual is at most `3.553e−15`, and
the independent `c1_difference_surplus` residual is at most `3.469e−14`.
Rebuilding the world tapes supplied the decision-boundary geometry; provider
array elevation and independent ECEF elevation agreed within `5.400e−13`
degrees.

[RUN] A subsequent direct traversal of `q1_schema_v2.build_exact_rows_v2` covers
all 37 modeled anchors. For each call the adapter sets
`PILOT_PRIMITIVE_SOURCE_FALLBACK = False` on the imported pilot module, invokes
the exact builder, and restores the original module value. Every regenerated
declared target, v1 parent Q1 vector, and v2 Q1 vector is compared row-for-row
with the fitted panel. The final direct-audit counts and maximum deviations are
reported in the reproducibility section below.

[RUN] Power is unchanged from v1: 37 independent anchor clusters contribute to
the anchor bootstrap. LOWO still has only two world clusters, so it is a
descriptive cross-world transfer check, not a powered population-of-worlds
estimate.

## Learner and metric protocol

[RUN] The sealed primary used the repository `AdamMLPHead` with the same literals:
hidden `(8,)`, ReLU, learning rate `1.0e-2`, `gauge_beta = 0.2`, Adam betas
`(0.9, 0.999)`, epsilon `1e-8`, zero weight decay, 2,000 source epochs, and one
deterministic full-batch update per route per epoch. The deterministic aggregate
retained 4,096 base-to-candidate pairs per fold. LOAO uses sealed learner seed
`6407676579069309528`; seeded LOWO results are arithmetic means over the same
four sealed seeds.

[RUN] Metric definitions are unchanged: raw held-out levels with no test-time
re-gauging for `R²`; all non-tied true pairs within held-out `(anchor,user)`
groups for ordering, with prediction ties worth `0.5`; and stable predicted
argmax matching any true maximum within `1e-12` for top-1.

